        "dependency_level": 1,
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $\\mathcal A$ be a smooth planar annulus and let $X=\\mathcal A\\times(-1,1)$, the interior of a solid torus. A regular annular diagram is a finite immersed union of circles in $\\operatorname{int}(\\mathcal A)$ with only finitely many transverse double points, no triple points, and over/under data from the interval coordinate. The **blackboard framing** is the normal strip lying in the annulus. A specified framing differs from it on each component by an integer full-twist correction, measured by the winding number of its normal direction relative to the blackboard normal. Count the correction positively for an A-type full twist, whose local curl has A-smoothing equal to the straight strip with one contractible circle; count the opposite B-type twist negatively. Every framed link in $X$ admits a regular annular diagram. The correction may be recorded as signed full-twist marks on diagram arcs; its total is additive when arcs are concatenated. If two framed links in $X$ are ambient isotopic through links in $X$, their regular annular diagrams are related by finitely many annular planar isotopies and local oriented Reidemeister I, II, and III moves in disks contained in $\\operatorname{int}(\\mathcal A)$. Reidemeister II and III and planar isotopy preserve the blackboard framing; an A-type or B-type Reidemeister I move changes it by one corresponding full twist, so the correction integer changes by the opposite twist. For framed links in $S^3$, every link also admits a regular planar diagram, and any two diagrams of ambient-isotopic framed links are related by a finite planar-isotopy/Reidemeister sequence with the same framing-correction rule.",
        "strategy": "Put link and isotopy projections in general position; use the local cusp, tangency, and triple-event models, then track the blackboard framing through each move.",
