        "dependency_level": 2,
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\mathcal A$ be a planar annulus and identify the solid torus with $\\mathcal A\\times[-1,1]$. Let $R=\\mathbb Z[A^{\\pm1}]$, $\\delta=-A^2-A^{-2}$, and let $z$ be a formal polynomial variable. For a regular annular diagram $D$, a state $S$ smooths every crossing. Let $a(S)$ and $b(S)$ count its A- and B-smoothings, let $u(S)$ count the resulting circles that bound disks in $\\mathcal A$, and let $e(S)$ count the resulting circles that do not bound disks in $\\mathcal A$; call a circle that does not bound a disk essential. If $m$ is the total full-twist correction from the blackboard framing to the specified framing, counting an A-type twist positively and its inverse negatively as in the framed diagram calculus, set $$ \\Psi(D,m)=(-A^3)^m\\sum_S A^{a(S)-b(S)}\\delta^{u(S)}z^{e(S)}. $$ This value is independent of the regular annular diagram and twist correction of the framed link and induces an $R$-linear map $$ \\Psi:\\mathcal K_A(\\mathcal A\\times[-1,1])\\longrightarrow R[z]. $$ For the empty link, the empty diagram has its unique empty state and the formula gives $\\Psi(\\varnothing)=1$. The diagram below shows one essential core curve and one contractible circle in an annular state; every smoothed component is classified by these two types.",
